---
// PILLAR — Charging It to the Game. Follows the index; followed by the contact
// section, which is the page's last content.
//
// ENTRANCE — this section is one of the two SANCTIONED EXCEPTIONS to the
// three-edge staircase, the other being the chapter break. Its eyebrow, its
// display heading, its lede, its body and its coda all hang on ONE edge,
// column 3, and that single unbroken edge is what makes it read differently
// from the five sections above it. Applying the staircase here as well would
// make the staircase its own kind of sameness, which is the failure mode
// CONTEXT.md names first.
//
// The index above opens on a gold hairline and hung display numerals and
// Contact below opens on a hairline head, so neither neighbour is matched.
//
// COLUMN WINDOW — A, the page default: columns 3-8. RHYTHM — open.
//
// IMAGE — promoted from `inset` to `column`, which is what CONTEXT.md's
// composition table specifies for this section. It was rendering at 330px in
// the margin alongside two other 330px insets, which is how the page ended up
// with three photographs at one width and no tier distinction at all. One
// inset now exists on the page, in Premier Leadership.
//
// NO OVERLAP HERE — TRIED, MEASURED, MOVED. The numeral was hung off the
// photograph's top-right corner, half on the picture and half on the page
// ground. Measured against the RENDERED pixels rather than the frame's
// average tone: Obsidian's worst contrast inside that box was 1.58:1, and no
// single colour can clear 4.5:1 against both Porcelain (needs a dark fill)
// and the frame's dark passages (needs a light one) — the two requirements
// are arithmetically exclusive. A probe of the whole frame on an 8x6 grid
// found no cell where the worst pixel cleared 4.5:1 against Obsidian, so it
// was not a matter of nudging it. CONTEXT.md's rule is that a numeral over a
// region it cannot hold moves, so it moved: it now hangs in the margin at the
// outer trim with the caption, like every other section numeral on the page.
// The page's third overlap went to the Everyday Legends lede instead.
//
// NO post index, NO fabricated posts, NO "coming soon". There are no articles
// and no blog URL. The copy is a manifesto and stands as one.
//
// The section element carries id="charging-it-to-the-game" so funnel index
// entry 05 has a real anchor available to be repointed at. That repointing is
// Jackson's call and is not this pass's scope.
//
// PHOTOGRAPH. Canonical source is brand_assets/SHP SHoot 7.jpg — the
// red-blazer downtown-street frame. Verified 2832x4240 with
// sharp().metadata(), no EXIF orientation flag, so the stored buffer is also
// the presented frame. VERTICAL, and not cropped wide. Inset crop cut from a
// figure-centred window:
//   extract left 700, top 980, 1500 x 2100   (5:7, three-quarter length)
// 5:7 is used nowhere else: the other frames run 3:4, 2:3 and 4:5.
//
// NO HAND-SET grid-column ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import lifestyleStreet from '../assets/lifestyle-street-inset.jpg';
---

<section class="blog spread" id="charging-it-to-the-game">
  <div class="blog-head col-body row-1">
    <p class="kicker">The Blog</p>
    <h2 class="blog-title">Charging It to the Game</h2>
  </div>

  <p class="lede col-body row-2">There are lessons that cannot be taught in a classroom, found in a playbook, or measured on a scoreboard.</p>

  <!-- .col-body, NOT .col-indent: the single edge is the point here. -->
  <div class="body-copy col-body row-3">
    <p>Charging It to the Game is where sports, family, leadership, and life meet&mdash;and where the experiences behind the accomplishments get a voice.</p>
    <p>Through personal stories, candid reflections, lessons learned, sacrifices made, wins celebrated, and losses absorbed, Dr.&nbsp;McClain shares the journey behind raising athletes while simultaneously navigating motherhood, marriage, education, leadership, business, and life.</p>

    <!-- The TURN. Isolated on both sides at the tight step and set in Obsidian
         against the Slate around it, so it can never be read as the tail of
         the paragraph above or the head of the one below. -->
    <p class="body-beat">But this is not simply a football story.</p>

    <p>It is about the miles driven when no one was watching. The sacrifices families make behind the scenes. The decisions that change trajectories. The relationships that shape people. The losses that teach more than victories ever could. And the understanding that sometimes the lesson only makes sense after living through it.</p>
    <p>For parents, athletes, educators, coaches, leaders, and anyone navigating a journey that does not come with a perfect playbook, Charging It to the Game creates space to tell the truth about the process, find meaning in the experience, and keep moving forward.</p>
  </div>

  <figure class="blog-figure col-body row-4 plate-img">
    <Image
      src={lifestyleStreet}
      alt="Dr. Syreeta McClain in a red blazer and jeans, standing on a downtown street"
      widths={[480, 720, 1000, 1500]}
      sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 48vw"
      quality={80}
      loading="eager"
      decoding="sync"
    />
  </figure>

  <!-- Live margin: the numeral, then the photograph's caption. Last in source
       order for this row, so both fall inline after the picture at 1280. -->
  <div class="blog-margin col-margin row-4">
    <span class="section-numeral section-numeral--head" aria-hidden="true">08</span>
    <p class="plate-caption">Red blazer, downtown street</p>
  </div>

  <!-- THE CODA. Three lines, typeset as three blocks. The line breaks are the
       design: they can wrap on a narrow screen but they can never run together
       into a sentence. Left-aligned, no rules between them. -->
  <p class="blog-coda col-coda row-5">
    <span class="coda-line">Because some things are wins.</span>
    <span class="coda-line">Some things are lessons.</span>
    <span class="coda-line">And some things you simply have to charge to the game.</span>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  /* RHYTHM: open. */
  .blog{ padding-top: var(--vr-open); }

  .blog-head{ margin-bottom: clamp(40px, 5vw, 72px); }

  /* Section-heading tier — opsz 96, wght 340, SOFT 32, WONK 0. Smaller than
     the lede below it: the lede is the section's display anchor and the title
     is the label on it, which is the rule everywhere on this page. */
  .blog-title{
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-display);
    font-weight: 340;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 2.8vw, 40px);
    line-height: 1.14;
    letter-spacing: -0.014em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* --- The column photograph --------------------------------------- */
  .blog-figure{
    /* Three baselines, not --vr-tight: the clamp lands on 96px at 1440 and
       96 is not a multiple of 28, so the figure and the margin block beside
       it would both start 12px off the lattice and the caption could not be
       snapped to the copy above. */
    margin-top: calc(3 * var(--baseline));
    /* 5:7 — a proportion used nowhere else on the page. */
    aspect-ratio: 5 / 7;
  }

  .blog-figure :global(img){
    object-position: 50% 40%;
    /* The red blazer is the most saturated area anywhere in the usable set and
       red sits outside the five locked hexes. Graded down hard so it lands as
       a muted clay inside the warm earth family rather than as a sixth colour;
       the same pass carries the sky and the yellow road paint down with it. */
    filter: grayscale(0.76) contrast(1.04) saturate(0.32) brightness(1.05);
  }

  /* NOT directional. The two athletics frames weight their gradient to one
     edge because their out-of-palette areas are at an edge — track underfoot,
     sky overhead. This frame's problem area is the RED BLAZER, which sits in
     the middle of the picture, so an edge-weighted wash misses it entirely and
     lands its gold on the sky instead, which is where the sepia cast came
     from. Gold is carried across the middle band and Cashmere closes both
     ends. Every stop is a locked palette value. */
  .blog-figure::before{
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom,
      var(--cashmere)  0%,
      var(--gold)     52%,
      var(--cashmere) 100%);
    opacity: 0.42;
  }

  /* Lighter than the shared default. The blazer is already the darkest thing
     in the frame and crushing it further pushes its relative saturation back
     UP, which is the opposite of what the grade above is for. */
  .blog-figure::after{
    background:
      linear-gradient(to top, rgba(26,22,19,0.22) 0%, rgba(26,22,19,0) 40%),
      linear-gradient(90deg,  rgba(209,199,189,0.30) 0%, rgba(209,199,189,0) 26%);
  }

  /* --- Live margin: numeral, then caption --------------------------- */
  .blog-margin{
    align-self: start;
    /* Snapped: the figure starts three baselines below the body and the
       margin block two further in, so the caption's baseline lands on a body
       baseline rather than on the figure's top. */
    margin-top: calc(5 * var(--baseline));
  }

  .blog-margin .section-numeral{ text-align: right; }

  .blog-margin .plate-caption{
    text-align: right;
    margin-top: var(--baseline);
    margin-left: 0;
  }

  /* --- The coda ----------------------------------------------------- */
  /* The OPEN step above. As with the education coda, the chapter break spends
     this step twice over, so it remains the page's largest gap. */
  .blog-coda{
    margin: var(--vr-open) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    /* Coda tier — opsz 48, wght 360, SOFT 45, WONK 0. */
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-coda);
    font-weight: 360;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.1vw, 30px);
    line-height: 1.52;
    letter-spacing: -0.008em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-align: left;      /* never centred */
  }

  /* Block-level, so the three lines are three lines structurally and not just
     visually. A wrapped line stays inside its own block; the three can never
     collapse into one paragraph. */
  .coda-line{
    display: block;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Generous leading BETWEEN the lines as well as within them — the cadence is
     the point, and three blocks at ordinary paragraph spacing would read as a
     single stanza. */
  .coda-line + .coda-line{ margin-top: 0.34em; }

  /* Inline in the measure: both go flush left again. */
  @media (max-width: 1279px){
    .blog-margin{ margin-top: var(--vr-tight); }
    .blog-margin .section-numeral,
    .blog-margin .plate-caption{ text-align: left; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px){
    /* Slightly tighter so the third line has a better chance of holding one
       line; if it still wraps that is acceptable, so long as it stays its own
       block, which .coda-line guarantees. */
    .blog-coda{ font-size: 19px; }
  }
</style>
